      "content": {
        "characters": [
          { "malayalam": "സ", "iso15919": "sa", "description": "Alveolar fricative. Like 's' in 'sun' — air flows continuously over the tongue tip. Very common in Sanskrit-origin Malayalam words.", "isNew": true },
          { "malayalam": "ഡ", "iso15919": "ḍa", "description": "Voiced retroflex stop. Like 'd' with the tongue curled back to touch the roof of the mouth. Appears mainly in loanwords and a few native forms.", "isNew": true }
        ],
        "insight": "സ (sa) is the familiar alveolar s-sound, common in Sanskrit loanwords. ഡ (ḍa) is the voiced partner of ട — same retroflex position, but voiced. You already know ട (unvoiced); ഡ adds the voice.",
